-- Q11520: Financial Reporting: For-Profit [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under SFAC No. 8 and IAS 16, the revaluation model permits upward adjustments to fair value, increasing relevance when market values diverge from historical cost. However, fair value estimates for non-financial assets require judgment, reducing verifiability. U.S. GAAP (ASC 360) prohibits revaluation, prioritizing verifiability and comparability.
Wrong (A): The revaluation model is not always preferable. For assets without observable market prices, reliability concerns and measurement uncertainty may outweigh relevance gains.
Wrong (B): Historical cost is not always preferable. It can significantly understate asset values for appreciated property, reducing the relevance of financial information.
Wrong (D): The two approaches do not produce equivalent information. Revaluation reflects current market conditions in the balance sheet, while historical cost defers gains until disposal.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11520;

-- Q11521: Financial Reporting: For-Profit [easy/L1]
